title op6: a surviving name's claim beats a lost set's

# The realization case the kernel lane found a real bug in, and the
# only fixture in the set that separates THREE implementations
# instead of two.
#
# Base holds one file under two names, x and y.  Onto does nothing.
# Off-of un-shares them: y stays on the original dnode and x becomes
# a separate object renamed to aa.  So the decision produces two
# output pools -- P1 holding {y}, and P2 holding {aa} with a delta of
# lost {x}, gained {aa}.
#
# Both pools want onto's dnode 10-100.  P1 wants it because y is a
# name onto still holds.  P2 wants it because its lost name x was on
# it.  They cannot both have it: two output pools reusing one onto
# dnode is what materialize exists to prevent, and an applier cannot
# make one inode be two files.
#
# THE SETTLED RULE, and why it needs two passes rather than a guard
# bolted onto one.  Realization resolves every NAME-DRIVEN answer
# first and claims as it goes, then runs the lost-set fallback -- with
# the type filter and the claimed check, both of which the fallback
# path needs exactly as much as the primary path does -- over
# whatever is still unresolved.  A pool holding a SURVIVING name has
# the better claim, and it must win regardless of where the two pools
# fall in the order the loop happens to walk.
#
# THREE IMPLEMENTATIONS, THREE DIFFERENT GOLD SECTIONS, which is what
# makes this fixture worth its size:
#
#   Two passes, guarded (settled, and the gold below).  y resolves to
#     10-100 and claims it.  aa falls to the fallback, finds 10-100
#     claimed, and materializes.  aa is the new object, which is what
#     the user did.
#
#   One pass in name order, guarded.  Names sort aa before y.  aa is
#     visited first, has no name-driven answer, takes 10-100 by the
#     lost-set route, and claims it.  y then finds it claimed and
#     materializes.  THE WRONG POOL MATERIALIZES -- the file that
#     never moved gets a new dnode, and the one that was split off
#     inherits the old one.  Same tree shape, inverted identity.
#
#   Unguarded.  Both take 10-100, so the split does not happen at all
#     and the output is one pool holding aa and y.  A different tree
#     shape, which is the only one of the three failures a gold
#     section reading names alone would catch.
#
# The middle case is the interesting one and the reason the loop
# changes shape in wave 2 rather than growing a condition: a guard
# alone makes the outcome depend on sort order, and sort order is not
# a fact about what the user did.
#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  none; no directory changed, /shared is green
#   deltas:          P2 lost {x}, gained {aa}.  P1 lost nothing --
#                    y survived, so it is not in any delta
#   decisions:       P1 {y} reuses onto's 10-100 by name-driven
#                    lookup, in pass one.  P2 {aa} materializes:
#                    pass two's lost-set route reaches 10-100 and
#                    the claimed guard refuses it
#   conflicts:       none.  Onto is quiet, so nothing is opposed;
#                    this is entirely a realization question
#   emission:        materialize aa, link it, unlink x

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: y

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: y

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      40-300: aa = v0
      10-100: y = v0

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared
      40-300: aa = v0
      10-100: y = v0
